@* Other random distributions.  We compute samples from some common random
distributions in batches to allow for vectorization.  The routines have
simpler interfaces than the uniform random number generator.  In
particular, there is no fast equivalents.

\ID{$Id$}

@m FILE 'randother.web'

@I macros.hweb

@I random.hweb

@* External interface.

Three distributions are provided: normal deviates, uniform points on
a sphere, and cosine-distributed points on a half sphere.  The calling
sequences are:

|x=rand_gauss(rn_args(tag))| returns a normal deviate
with zero mean and unit variance.

|call rand_isodist(v,rn_args(tag))| returns a point
uniformly distributed on a sphere in the array |v[3]|.

|call rand_cosdist(v,rn_args(tag))| returns a point
cosine-distributed on a sphere in the array |v[3]|.


@a
      @<Functions and Subroutines@>

@* Normal random numbers. Generate normal random numbers with zero mean and
unit variance.  This is the Box-Muller method \cite{box58} described in
Knuth \cite{knuth2}, p.~117--118.  However, rather than use the rejection
method to calculate $\cos\Theta$ and $\sin\Theta$, we calculate them
directly, to allow vectorization.

Because of the way the uniform random numbers are generated, this routine
will never attempt to take $\log 0$.  In addition, $0$ will never be one of
the normal random numbers generated.  (These observations follow because
|random| never returns $0$, $\frac 14$, $\frac 12$, $\frac 34$, or $1$.)

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine gauss_batch(rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none
      rn_decl(x)                // Input/Output
      integer i                 // Local
      real pi,theta,z
      external random_check,fast_random // External
      real fast_random

      pi=PI
      call random_check(RAN_G,rn_args(x))
      do i=0,RAN_G-1,2
         theta=pi*(two*fast_random(rn_args(x))-one) // uniformly distributed in $(-\pi,\pi)$
         z=sqrt(-two*log(fast_random(rn_args(x)))) // $\sqrt{-2\log S}$
         rn_gauss_array(x)[i]=z*cos(theta)
         rn_gauss_array(x)[i+1]=z*sin(theta)
      end do

      rn_gauss_index(x)=RAN_G         // The count of the available random numbers

      return
      end

@ Get next element from |rn_gauss_array(x)|.

@<Functions...@>=
      function rand_gauss(rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none
      real rand_gauss           // Function
      rn_decl(x)                // Input/Ouput

      if ( rn_gauss_index(x) <= 0 ) call gauss_batch(rn_args(x))
      rn_gauss_index(x) = rn_gauss_index(x) - 1 // Decrement the count
      rand_gauss = rn_gauss_array(x)[rn_gauss_index(x)] // Return top element of |rn_gauss_array(x)|

      return
      end

@* Isotropic distribution.  Generate random points isotropically on a
three-dimensional unit sphere.  Each of the coordinates is uniformly
distributed $[-1,1]$.  We use this to obtain the $z$ coordinate.  Then $x$
and $y$ are uniformly distributed on a circle of radius $\sqrt{1-z^2}$.
See Carter and Cashwell \cite{carter75}, p.~7.  There are rejection
techniques to calculate these random numbers, but once again the additional
complexity will prevent vectorization, so these techniques are unlikely to
be faster.

Because of the way the uniform random numbers are calculated, this routine
will never return $0$ for any of the components of the position.  (However,
it {\it may} return $1$ for $x$ or $y$ components.)

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine isodist_batch(rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none
      rn_decl(x)                // Input/Output
      integer i                 // Local
      real pi,costheta,phi
      external random_check,fast_random // External
      real fast_random

      pi=PI
      call random_check(2*RAN_I,rn_args(x))
      do i=0,RAN_I-1
         costheta=two*fast_random(rn_args(x))-one // uniformly distributed in $(-1,1)$
         phi=pi*(two*fast_random(rn_args(x))-one) // uniformly distributed in $(-\pi,\pi)$
         rn_iso_array(x)[1,i]=cos(phi)*sqrt(one-costheta^2)
         rn_iso_array(x)[2,i]=sin(phi)*sqrt(one-costheta^2)
         rn_iso_array(x)[3,i]=costheta
      end do

      rn_iso_index(x)=RAN_I

      return
      end

@ Get next element from |rn_iso_array(x)|.

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine rand_isodist(v,rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none
      real v[3]                 // Output
      rn_decl(x)                // Input/Ouput

      if ( rn_iso_index(x) <= 0 ) call isodist_batch(rn_args(x))
      rn_iso_index(x) = rn_iso_index(x) - 1 // Decrement the count
      vc_copy(rn_iso_array(x)[rn_iso_index(x)],v) // Return the top element of |rn_iso_array(x)|

      return
      end

@* Cosine distribution.  Generate random points with a cosine distribution
on a three-dimensional unit sphere.  This distribution is biased
$\cos\theta$ relative to the isotropic distribution, where $\theta$ is the
angle measured from the $z$ axis.  This is the distribution of directions
for thermal particles coming off a surface--the factor of $\cos\theta$
accounting for the solid angle subtended by a surface element.

If particles are emitted into a volume from its enclosing surface uniformly
in position and with this distribution in direction, then each point in the
volume is visited equally often and each direction is sampled uniformly.

The probablilty of a particle lying in $[\theta,\theta+d\theta]$ is
$\frac12 \sin\theta \,d\theta$ for $\theta$ in $[-\frac12 \pi,\frac12 \pi]$
in the isotropic case.  For the cosine distribution the probability is $2
\cos\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta$ for $\theta$ in $[0,\frac12 \pi]$.  The
corresponding distribution in $z$ is $f(z)=2z$ for $0\le z\le 1$, and the
cumulative distribution in $F(z)=z^2$.  We can generate this distribution
using $Z=F^{-1}(U)=\sqrt U$ where $U$ is a uniformly distributed random
number.  The standard ``trick'' to calculate $\sqrt U$ is to take
$\max(U_1,U_2)$.  However, once again, it seems to be of marginal value to
resort to these tricks.  Having computed $z$, we obtain $x$ and $y$ using
the same method as for the isotropic distribution.

Because of the way the uniform random numbers are calculated, this routine
will never return $0$ for any of the components of the position.  (However,
it {\it may} return $1$ for any of the components.)

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine cosdist_batch(rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none
      rn_decl(x)                // Input/Output
      integer i                 // Local
      real pi,costheta2,phi
      external random_check,fast_random // External
      real fast_random

      pi=PI
      call random_check(2*RAN_C,rn_args(x))
      do i=0,RAN_C-1
         costheta2=fast_random(rn_args(x)) // $\cos^2\theta$
         phi=pi*(two*fast_random(rn_args(x))-one) // uniformly distributed in $(-\pi,\pi)$
         rn_cos_array(x)[3,i]=sqrt(costheta2) // distributed as $z^2$ in $(-0,1)$
         rn_cos_array(x)[1,i]=cos(phi)*sqrt(one-costheta2)
         rn_cos_array(x)[2,i]=sin(phi)*sqrt(one-costheta2)
      end do

      rn_cos_index(x)=RAN_I

      return
      end

@ Get next element from |rn_cos_array(x)|.

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine rand_cosdist(v,rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none
      real v[3]                 // Output
      rn_decl(x)                // Input/Ouput

      if ( rn_cos_index(x) <= 0 ) call cosdist_batch(rn_args(x))
      rn_cos_index(x) = rn_cos_index(x) - 1 // Decrement the count
      vc_copy(rn_cos_array(x)[rn_cos_index(x)],v) // Return the top element of |rn_cos_array(x)|

      return
      end
      
